WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.998
Two retrievers have the same architecture

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.163
and search the same collection.

00:00:05.338 --> 00:00:07.959
One learnt from question and answer pairs.

00:00:08.122 --> 00:00:10.318
The other learnt from citation-linked

00:00:10.338 --> 00:00:12.818
papers. Should they produce the same

00:00:12.838 --> 00:00:14.686
ranking? Make a prediction.

00:00:17.107 --> 00:00:18.904
They can learn different relationships

00:00:18.924 --> 00:00:21.275
because their training examples reward

00:00:21.295 --> 00:00:22.434
different things.

00:00:22.622 --> 00:00:25.038
The targets and negative examples give a

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:27.409
retriever an operational model of

00:00:27.429 --> 00:00:30.333
relevance. To understand its high scores,

00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:32.215
ask where that lesson came from.

00:00:33.141 --> 00:00:35.186
A clicked result records an action.

00:00:35.348 --> 00:00:37.764
Clicks can reward popularity and ranking

00:00:37.784 --> 00:00:40.096
position as well as usefulness.

00:00:40.258 --> 00:00:42.468
A question paired with an answer passage

00:00:42.488 --> 00:00:44.749
instead rewards an answer-bearing

00:00:44.769 --> 00:00:47.197
relationship. Neither source directly

00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:49.542
observes everything a searcher means by

00:00:49.562 --> 00:00:50.547
relevance.

00:00:51.113 --> 00:00:53.631
Titles paired with abstracts may mainly

00:00:53.651 --> 00:00:55.679
reward topical similarity.

00:00:55.893 --> 00:00:58.772
Human judgements, generated queries and

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:01.066
neighbouring passages can supply other

00:01:01.086 --> 00:01:02.354
training signals.

00:01:02.504 --> 00:01:05.035
Each has advantages and limitations.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.484
Knowing the signal helps you ask which

00:01:07.504 --> 00:01:09.752
relationship a model was encouraged to

00:01:09.772 --> 00:01:10.627
preserve.

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:13.813
The chapter gives SPECTER as a deliberate

00:01:13.833 --> 00:01:15.281
scholarly example.

00:01:15.431 --> 00:01:18.169
It trains document-level paper embeddings

00:01:18.189 --> 00:01:19.946
using the citation graph.

00:01:20.109 --> 00:01:22.563
Citation-linked relationships shape a

00:01:22.583 --> 00:01:25.218
space intended to reflect scholarly

00:01:25.238 --> 00:01:26.261
relatedness.

00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:28.873
That is a different target from finding a

00:01:28.893 --> 00:01:30.690
direct answer to a question.

00:01:30.852 --> 00:01:33.280
Relatedness can be useful, but it does not

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.883
prove that a paper answers this search,

00:01:35.994 --> 00:01:38.409
supports another paper's conclusion, or

00:01:38.429 --> 00:01:41.050
meets a review's eligibility criteria.

00:01:41.213 --> 00:01:43.332
The training relationship needs to fit the

00:01:43.352 --> 00:01:44.246
use.

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:47.042
A retriever trained on general web

00:01:47.062 --> 00:01:49.374
question and answer pairs learns from that

00:01:49.394 --> 00:01:52.363
literature, genre and style of question.

00:01:52.526 --> 00:01:54.787
A systematic reviewer, a chemistry

00:01:54.807 --> 00:01:57.622
researcher, or someone searching another

00:01:57.642 --> 00:01:59.941
language may need distinctions barely

00:01:59.961 --> 00:02:01.848
represented in those examples.

00:02:02.385 --> 00:02:04.454
Changing the examples can change what

00:02:04.474 --> 00:02:06.425
ranks highly even with the same

00:02:06.445 --> 00:02:09.312
architecture. A strong benchmark result

00:02:09.332 --> 00:02:12.083
may transfer poorly to another collection

00:02:12.103 --> 00:02:13.680
or query population.

00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:16.232
Treat transfer as something to evaluate

00:02:16.252 --> 00:02:18.758
locally, not as a guarantee supplied by a

00:02:18.778 --> 00:02:19.698
model name.

00:02:20.622 --> 00:02:23.098
If exact identifiers rarely matter during

00:02:23.118 --> 00:02:25.842
training, an embedding may blur them.

00:02:25.992 --> 00:02:28.807
If negatives rarely contain contradictions

00:02:28.827 --> 00:02:32.299
or specialised senses, the space may fail

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:34.373
to preserve those distinctions.

00:02:34.548 --> 00:02:38.188
Our invented labels C M P one zero four

00:02:38.208 --> 00:02:41.474
and C M P one four zero illustrate an

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:42.904
identifier distinction.

00:02:43.475 --> 00:02:45.639
These are teaching identifiers, not

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:47.971
chemical facts or a model test.

00:02:48.095 --> 00:02:50.368
A useful evaluation should include the

00:02:50.388 --> 00:02:53.178
exact identifiers, relations,

00:02:53.198 --> 00:02:55.858
contradictions and specialised senses that

00:02:55.878 --> 00:02:57.662
matter to your real task.

00:02:57.824 --> 00:03:00.819
Broad topical similarity alone cannot

00:03:00.839 --> 00:03:02.327
settle those cases.

00:03:03.238 --> 00:03:05.914
Suppose the web-trained retriever performs

00:03:05.934 --> 00:03:08.374
poorly over a chemistry collection.

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.385
Training mismatch is a plausible

00:03:10.405 --> 00:03:13.555
hypothesis. Before attributing the failure

00:03:13.575 --> 00:03:16.377
to training, inspect collection coverage,

00:03:16.397 --> 00:03:19.161
searchable fields, query processing and

00:03:19.181 --> 00:03:20.629
candidate cut-offs.

00:03:21.176 --> 00:03:23.455
Then compare performance across a local

00:03:23.475 --> 00:03:25.516
set of judged queries.

00:03:25.678 --> 00:03:27.875
The scenario says how retrieval training

00:03:27.895 --> 00:03:30.620
was supplied; it does not tell you whether

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.526
domain pretraining occurred.

00:03:32.689 --> 00:03:35.336
Different stages can produce the same

00:03:35.408 --> 00:03:37.450
missing-paper symptom, and neither

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:40.013
training lever alone guarantees a fit.

00:03:40.926 --> 00:03:43.468
Take three questions back to the library.

00:03:43.644 --> 00:03:45.827
What text unit does each stored vector

00:03:45.847 --> 00:03:48.804
represent? Which positives, negatives and

00:03:48.824 --> 00:03:51.497
training signals taught its comparisons?

00:03:51.646 --> 00:03:53.688
Does it retrieve useful material for our

00:03:53.708 --> 00:03:57.089
own task, collection and query population?

00:03:57.653 --> 00:04:00.392
Model tokenisation supplies inputs.

00:04:00.554 --> 00:04:03.446
Contextual encoding and pooling produce a

00:04:03.466 --> 00:04:04.619
representation.

00:04:04.768 --> 00:04:06.423
Retrieval training shapes which

00:04:06.443 --> 00:04:08.536
comparisons score highly.

00:04:08.686 --> 00:04:10.483
Local evidence tests whether those

00:04:10.503 --> 00:04:12.338
comparisons serve your need.

00:04:12.513 --> 00:04:15.135
Chapter Seven puts the encoder to work

00:04:15.155 --> 00:04:17.286
across a whole collection.
